Hello, thanks for your response. Prior to your message, I messed around with the `takeoff_async` and noticed that it can still fail/ throw errors but at least it doesn't throw a seg-fault (so you can handle the cases where this happens):

This code snippet is a little weird in implementation because I am turning an async method into a blocking one but this works and "gets the job done" so to speak. This one doesn't throw errors because everything stays in scope until the takeoff result is returned, thread notified etc. (if you use it perhaps put a timeout or something....).
```cpp
void MyClass::takeoffSync(mavsdk::Action::Result& result)
{
    LOG_INFO("Mavlink::takeoffSync() Method called!");
    std::condition_variable cv;
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    bool done = false;

    // Takeoff is an async function, so we need to wait for it to complete before returning
    action->takeoff_async([&](mavsdk::Action::Result tempResult) { 
        result = tempResult;
        done = true;
        cv.notify_one();
    });
    
    // Wait until the async function has completed...
    cv.wait(lock, [&]{ return done; });
}
```

